---
import "../styles/global.css";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Nazare is a semantic mutation substrate for autonomous software engineering."
    />
    <title>Nazare — A Semantic Mutation Substrate</title>
  </head>
  <body class="min-h-svh bg-[#f1f1ee] font-mono text-[#171717] antialiased dark:bg-[#151515] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]">
    <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-5 py-12 sm:px-8 sm:py-20 lg:py-28">
      <article aria-labelledby="title">
        <h1 id="title" class="max-w-2xl text-xl leading-[1.35] font-semibold tracking-[-0.025em] sm:text-2xl">
          Nazare: A Semantic Mutation Substrate for Autonomous Software Engineering
        </h1>

        <div class="mt-10 space-y-6 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.7] tracking-[-0.012em] text-[#555651] sm:text-base dark:text-[#aaa9a4]">
          <p>
            AI coding agents generate code efficiently but lack a durable model for governing
            software evolution. Existing approaches reconstruct architecture from source code,
            search for affected dependencies, and validate resulting patches. They do not maintain
            an authoritative semantic state against which intended changes can be executed and
            reconciled.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nazare introduces a transactional <span class="text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]">Mutation API</span> over a persistent semantic map of a
            codebase. Rather than attempting a complete representation of program behavior, Nazare
            maintains an evolving graph of <span class="text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]">Behavioral Regions</span>—inferred change units whose
            implementations must evolve together to modify a behavior safely—along with their
            dependencies, contracts, effects, and product surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Agents express intended semantic changes <span class="text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]">Δ</span> against durable state <span class="text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]">S<sub>t</sub></span>. Nazare identifies
            the affected region graph, generates candidate implementations, verifies semantic
            obligations, and reconciles the observed codebase against the expected transition:
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto py-3 text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]" role="img" aria-label="S sub t plus delta, mutation to observed S sub t plus 1, reconciliation to S sub t plus 1">
            <div class="flex min-w-max items-center justify-center gap-3 px-3 text-sm sm:gap-4 sm:text-base">
              <span>S<sub>t</sub> + Δ</span>
              <span class="flex flex-col items-center leading-none">
                <span class="text-[0.6875rem] text-[#777873] dark:text-[#8d8e89]">mutation</span>
                <span aria-hidden="true">⟶</span>
              </span>
              <span>S<sub>t+1</sub><sup class="ml-1 text-[0.65em]">observed</sup></span>
              <span class="flex flex-col items-center leading-none">
                <span class="text-[0.6875rem] text-[#777873] dark:text-[#8d8e89]">reconcile</span>
                <span aria-hidden="true">⟶</span>
              </span>
              <span>S<sub>t+1</sub></span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Its <span class="text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]">Wind Tunnel</span> evaluates valid implementations against plausible future mutations,
            optimizing not merely immediate correctness but the expected cost of subsequent
            software evolution:
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto py-3 text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]" role="img" aria-label="c star equals arg min over valid c of expected future mutation cost given c">
            <div class="min-w-max px-3 text-center text-sm sm:text-base">
              c* = arg min<sub class="ml-1">c ∈ C<sub>valid</sub></sub>
              E<sub>δ ∼ P(Δ<sub>future</sub>)</sub>
              [Cost(δ | c)]
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Each mutation produces evidence about how implementation decisions affect future
            changes, creating the foundation for a learned model of software evolution.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unlike systems that repeatedly infer architecture from successive codebase snapshots,
            Nazare preserves semantic identity and mutation history across transitions. Its
            competitive hypothesis is that this persistent, reconciled state enables cheaper change
            selection, more exhaustive coverage of modeled obligations, bounded execution, and
            cumulative architectural optimization.
          </p>

          <p class="text-[#171717] dark:text-[#e8e8e3]">
            Nazare is not another coding agent. It is an operating substrate for agent-maintained
            software, designed to make codebase mutations transactional, verifiable, and
            increasingly optimized for future change—the foundation for lights-off software
            engineering.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
